A||N||14||sjp777||subwoofer alternative||||1||
Z||000000||sjp777||04-04-2001||11:48 PM||||Rather than dick around looking for a stock sub assembly, I went a different route. Q-logic 8" truck box with a Rockford Fosgate 8" sub  and 200w bridged for mono amp behind the passenger seat. Both the sub and the box are at Best buy for about $110 for both. Amp ran another $100 or so. For the Fiero's small passenger compartment, it's all you need.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1629836&a=12458590&p=45776420&Sequence=0&res=high">           <BR><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black88GT||04-04-2001||11:51 PM||||Question, did you just drill the amp into the firewall to mount it?  I am wondering if I should do this or tear up the carpet and put a piece of wood under there.  How long were the screws?||||reg||5
Z||000002||sjp777||04-04-2001||11:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Question, did you just drill the amp into the firewall to mount it?  I am wondering if I should do this or tear up the carpet and put a piece of wood under there.  How long were the screws?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just used 2" long self tapping screws and went directly into the firewall. I know it should be mounted on wood for electrical noise factors, but since the amp is set for only low frequencies for the sub, I didn't think it would be a problem. I was right, no noise, just clean bass.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||04-05-2001||12:34 AM||||How far forward does the passenger seat have to sit to accomodate this? Could you post a pic with the seat back?<P>I would like to put one behind each seat but I don't want to sacrifice too much comfort.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||04-05-2001||01:12 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fyerstarter:<BR><B>How far forward does the passenger seat have to sit to accomodate this? Could you post a pic with the seat back?<P>I would like to put one behind each seat but I don't want to sacrifice too much comfort.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't have a picture right now, but I'll work on it. The seat needs to move up about 3" from all the way back. On the passenger side, without the pedals, this is hardly noticably in terms of legroom. There is plenty on that side, unless your pasenger is over 6'5". With the seat reclined all the way back, you can't even tell it is there. Usually, I keep it all the way back like that, but the majority of people who sit on that side want to move the seat up when they get in. The space you lose is not worth worrying about. You, or your passenger won't even notice. On the drivers side, I don't know. I like my seat pretty much all the way back. Bass is omni-directional. It doesn't need to be in stereo. For that small of space, you only need one 8 for kickin bass. If you really feel that's not enough, you'd be better off with one 10" box and a 300 or 400 watt amp. My stereo with one 8 sounds better than just about every SUV with twin 12's that I have ever heard. It's all about passenger compartement volume, and the Fiero has very little to fill.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||04-05-2001||03:32 AM||||Thanks for the info. I like my bass nice and tight anyway rather than some big booming 15's but I don't know if only one 8 would cut it :\  I guess if it's not enough I can always do a stimpy style 10" box in the footwell too [evil grin] &gt;:\||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||04-05-2001||09:15 AM||||I agree that a good 8" sub with 100W or more is plenty of good bass for a Fiero unless you want to be heard one block away (many do!). I just have an 8" RF with 120W and no one has told me that it is not enough. That setup is good with cloth seats but I'm not sure if it will hear the same with leather. I remember reading in Auto Sound & Security mag a guy with subs in the trunk that after adding a leather interior the bass diminished significantly due to the better sealing properties of leather.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||04-05-2001||09:27 AM||||That cracks me up that passengers would pull the seat up. Is that to make room for the people in the back? Silly rabbits. <BR>I'd be interested in hearing how a set-up like this sounds. Unfortunately, it would never work for someone like me. At my size (6'6") having the passenger forward does a nice block on my vision to the right. Oh well. But  I also wonder if some of the concussion of crisp bass is diminished by having a seatback in front of it.<BR>Oh, and your amp looks like the same Phase Linear 200w that I have driving my Pioneer 4x10s. Decent little amp for the money. <P>fyerstarter, I'm getting ready to start on a new box.  Ya know, I can make it to hold 2 8"ers. I got the hook-up, homes.<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 04-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||RacinRob||04-05-2001||11:04 AM||||sjp777 could you post the dimensions of your box. I have something similar, but I have a huge box behind the seat and anyone that sits in the car is uncomfortable. I want to put my 8" in a smaller sealed box, but I need the size of one first, that I know fits well.  I have an 8" MTX road thunder pro and a MTX 240 thunder amp, a nice pioneer head unit and pioneer front speakers in my 85 gt and it sounds nice!||||reg||1
Z||000009||sjp777||04-05-2001||08:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B> Unfortunately, it would never work for someone like me. At my size (6'6") having the passenger forward does a nice block on my vision to the right. <P>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 04-05-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, because of the angle and height of the box, the seat can still be reclined all the way back so the headrest is only about 3" off the firewall. The seat is well back from the forward edge of the pillar where the rear speaker is. When it's all the way back like that, you can't even tell something is back there. No vision obstruction out the side window.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sjp777||04-05-2001||08:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RacinRob:<BR><B>sjp777 could you post the dimensions of your box. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Box is 14" high by 15" wide. Depth at the base is 6 1/2", at the top 4 1/2" ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||04-06-2001||08:23 AM||||I don't understand. If the bottom of the box is 6.5" how come the set has to be moved only 3" forward? Maybe my car is different...||||reg||1
Z||000012||RacinRob||04-06-2001||10:49 AM||||how many cubic feet is it, I don't have my physics book with me to figure out the volume of a wedge.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||04-06-2001||11:18 AM||||The "Q" box is .35 cu ft. (internal volume with speaker) I have one. (if someone wants it let me know! I sell it for a real good price!) It causes the seat to be 4 clicks forward not 3". It's about 5" away from the firewall at the base of the seat. The headrest is 3" away. For me that is too far forward. So I am designing and building a subwoofer box that will go in the stock location. Also with leather seats an 8" gets muffled when the seat is against it. (I tried it first and did not like the sound) Cloth seats it would be better since air passes thru cloth better. I am using a Rockford Fosgate 8" with 170W and sitting in the box it sounds VERY good. I am only 6'1" and I didn't like sitting in the passenger seat with it that far forward. The more forward the seat the more chances that the passenger bumps the dash edge by the vent. Mine are not cracked and I don't want them cracked.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||sjp777||04-06-2001||08:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>I don't understand. If the bottom of the box is 6.5" how come the set has to be moved only 3" forward? Maybe my car is different...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I said the head rest is only 3" forward. Box is angled, as is the firewall, and seat reclines back. I don't have the box sitting on the floor either. It is screwed to the firewall and sits a little off the floor so that the bottom corner of the box at it's deepest point fits just under the seat below the hinge point. This gives me about another inch of room for the seat to move back but doesn't effect the recline. From the fully forward seat position, I can move the seat back 4 full clicks, and the seat will recline back to where it just brushes the amp mounted above the box.<P>Jeez, some of you seem to be a little skeptical. I'll post some pictures this weekend with the seat back and you can see for yourselves<BR>||||reg||1
